{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Refined Prototype and Capstone Defense","title":"Peer Scholarly Critique","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting peer scholarly critique to peer-critique-and-disposition log (criterion-anchored critique points plus accept / modify / reject dispositions), reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-01","statement":"Convert praise or preference into criterion-anchored, actionable peer critique using the element-problem-criterion frame"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-02","statement":"Respond to each critique point with a reasoned disposition (accept, modify, or reject), including at least one reasoned rejection"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-03","statement":"Verify an AI-drafted critique against the actual artifact before adopting any of its points"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_refined_prototype_and_capstone_defense","peer_review","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"06","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/06/05"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_annotation_signal"],"ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_calibration"],"ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_artifact_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Peer Scholarly Critique\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Peer Review, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_refined_prototype_and_capstone_defense\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/06-06-peer-scholarly-critique.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Peer Scholarly Critique: Peer Review, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\n\"Great module, really clear, 5 out of 5\" is not a critique; it is applause. Scholarly critique is a distinct genre with rules borrowed from academic peer review: every claim about the work must be anchored to a stated criterion, separated from personal preference, and phrased as something the author can act on. The skill cuts both ways. As a reviewer you learn to produce feedback that improves a peer's work; as an author you learn to receive criticism and respond to each point with a reasoned disposition rather than defensiveness or blanket compliance.\n\nThis matters because design is a social, reviewed practice. Real instructional products pass through stakeholder review, SME critique, and accessibility audit before launch, and a designer who cannot give actionable feedback or respond to it with judgment will struggle in any of those rooms. The capstone peer critique is a rehearsal for that professional reality, which is why both roles are graded.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Convert praise or preference into criterion-anchored, actionable peer critique using the element-problem-criterion frame.\n- Respond to each critique point with a reasoned disposition (accept, modify, or reject), including at least one reasoned rejection.\n- Verify an AI-drafted critique against the actual artifact before adopting any of its points.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA reviewer writes, \"Great module, really clear and engaging. I would have used a different color for the buttons though. 5 out of 5.\" The author cannot use a word of it. The praise names no criterion the author could try to preserve, and the one suggestion is the reviewer's personal taste presented as if it were a quality judgment. The review is simultaneously too kind to be useful and too arbitrary to be acted on.\n\nA scholarly version of the same review would anchor each point to a criterion the author already accepts, an objective-to-assessment alignment gap, a missing caption, and would flag the button color explicitly as preference, scoring it on nothing. The author can then act on the criterion-based points and reasonably set the preference aside. That separation, criterion from taste, is the whole craft.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nScholarly critique is **criterion-anchored, actionable feedback**, and it has a structure on the giving side and a structure on the receiving side. Both are assessable, because a critique only improves an artifact if the author can metabolize it.\n\nOn the giving side, each point has three parts:\n\n- **Element:** the specific screen, item, or component being critiqued, so the author knows where to look.\n- **Problem:** the observable issue with that element.\n- **Criterion:** the standard the problem violates (alignment, accessibility, signaling), which converts an opinion into a reviewable claim.\n\nMatch each move in the critique exchange to the role and purpose it serves. The giving side names problems against criteria; the receiving side owes each point a reasoned disposition.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Element\",\"definition\":\"Giving: names the specific screen, item, or component so the author knows where to look\"},{\"term\":\"Criterion\",\"definition\":\"Giving: the standard the problem violates, converting an opinion into a reviewable claim\"},{\"term\":\"Preference (flagged)\",\"definition\":\"Giving: a personal taste labeled as such and excluded from scoring\"},{\"term\":\"Disposition\",\"definition\":\"Receiving: an accept, reject, or modify with a reason for each point\"},{\"term\":\"Reasoned reject\",\"definition\":\"Receiving: declining a point on a criterion you already meet, the strongest evidence of judgment\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThe non-negotiable move is separating criterion from preference. A reviewer who dislikes a color may say so, but must label it as preference and not score it, because preferences are not violations. This is the same discipline a journal reviewer uses when distinguishing \"this method is inappropriate for the question\" (a criterion-based objection) from \"I would have framed the introduction differently\" (a stylistic preference the author may ignore).\n\nOn the receiving side, the author owes each point a **disposition**: accept (with the change made), reject (with a reason), or modify. Blanket acceptance of all feedback is as weak as blanket rejection; both skip the judgment that the response is meant to demonstrate. A reasoned reject (\"the current color already meets the contrast standard, so I keep it\") is stronger evidence of design maturity than an automatic accept, because it shows the author evaluated the point against a criterion rather than deferring to the reviewer's authority. UDL and Mayer's principles supply the shared criteria that let reviewer and author argue on common ground instead of trading tastes.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nHere is one peer comment on a classmate's prototype, written two ways. Read the before version, then the annotated after version.\n\n**Before (weak critique):**\n\n> Great module, really clear and engaging. I would have used a different color for the buttons though. 5/5.\n\nThat is praise plus a personal preference. The author cannot act on it because no criterion is named. Now the same reviewer's feedback rebuilt as scholarly critique:\n\n**After (annotated critique, with the author's disposition):**\n\n1. **Element + problem + criterion:** Module 2 states an \"evaluate\" objective, but its only activity is a matching item that asks learners to recall definitions. This fails the objective-to-assessment alignment criterion.\n2. **Element + problem + criterion:** The narrated video on screen 3 has no captions, which fails the accessibility criterion for time-based media.\n3. **Preference flagged as preference:** The button color is a personal taste, not a criterion, so I am noting it separately and not scoring it.\n\nThe author then logs a disposition for each point:\n\n- Point 1: **Accept.** Replaced the matching item with a scenario-judgment task that requires evaluation.\n- Point 2: **Accept.** Added captions to screen 3.\n- Point 3: **Reject.** The current color meets the contrast standard, so I keep it.\n\nThe after version names elements, problems, and criteria the author can act on, separates preference from criterion, and shows reasoned judgment on each point rather than blanket acceptance.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTest feedback from both sides before you trust the exchange. A critique only counts if it is actionable; a response only counts if it shows judgment.\n\n1. **Actionability test (giving):** Could the author act on each point without asking what you meant? If a point has no element or no criterion, it is not yet usable.\n2. **Separation test (giving):** Is every preference labeled as preference and excluded from scoring?\n3. **Disposition test (receiving):** Does each point get an accept, reject, or modify with a reason, rather than a blanket response?\n\nIf any test fails, the exchange is applause or compliance, not scholarly critique. Add the missing element, criterion, or disposition.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A reviewer writes: &quot;Screen 4's narrated clip has no captions, which fails the accessibility criterion for time-based media. Also I would have used a serif font.&quot; Which part is criterion-anchored and which is preference?\" explanation=\"The caption point names an element (screen 4), a problem (no captions), and a criterion (accessibility for time-based media), so it is actionable. The serif font is a personal taste with no criterion attached and must be labeled as preference and excluded from scoring.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The caption point is criterion-anchored; the font is preference and should not be scored\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Both are criterion-anchored\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Both are preferences because they are one reviewer opinion\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The font is the criterion; captions are preference\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is the praise sandwich performed for politeness, where the criterion-based problem is buried so gently between compliments that the author misses it. Kindness is not the goal; actionability is. State the problem and its criterion plainly, and let the criterion, not the softening, carry the respect.\n\nA second error, on the author's side, is treating every reviewer point as a command. Accepting all feedback uncritically produces a Frankenstein artifact that satisfies each reviewer and serves no learner, and it forfeits the judgment the response is meant to show. The strongest responses include at least one reasoned reject, because declining a point on a criterion proves you are evaluating feedback rather than obeying it.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWhen you review a peer, hold the rubric beside their artifact and force every comment through the element-problem-criterion frame. If you cannot name the criterion a comment violates, you have a preference, and you either drop it or label it as preference and move on. Aim for a small number of criterion-anchored points the author can act on rather than a long list that mixes substance with taste.\n\nWhen you receive a review, resist the urge to defend or to comply. Take each point and ask what criterion it invokes, then decide: does my artifact actually violate that criterion? If yes, accept and make the change. If no, reject with the criterion that shows you already meet the standard. Write the disposition next to the point so a committee can see not just what you changed but what you declined and why. The walkthrough ends with a critique that improved the work and a response log that demonstrates judgment in both directions.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, practice both roles on a real example: rewrite one piece of praise you have received into a criterion-anchored point, and draft a reasoned reject for one suggestion you disagree with. Strong reading here means recognizing that the hardest move in critique is the honest reject, because it requires you to name the criterion that justifies keeping your choice.\n\nA strong reader of peer scholarly critique can do four things:\n\n1. Anchor every critique point to an element and a criterion.\n2. Separate preference from criterion and score only the latter.\n3. Respond to each point with an accept, reject, or modify and a reason.\n4. Produce at least one reasoned reject rather than blanket compliance.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong critique exchange is judged by whether the feedback was actionable and the response showed judgment. On the giving side, the standard fails when a review is praise plus taste; on the receiving side, it fails when every point is accepted without evaluation. The artifact you submit is the round trip: the critique you gave, the dispositions you logged, and the criterion-based reasoning on both. When you are unsure whether a point is criterion or preference, annotate it and request a comparison between a criterion-anchored critique and a preference-driven one.\n\nA final paragraph for the artifact would read like this: \"I gave criterion-anchored, actionable feedback and labeled my preferences as preferences. When I received feedback I logged a disposition for every point, accepting the alignment and accessibility fixes and rejecting one point with the criterion that showed I already met the standard.\"\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"peer_review\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"ail606_supplement_refined_prototype_and_capstone_defense\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the exchange into a workplace review with a power asymmetry, for example feedback from a client or a senior stakeholder rather than a peer. The criterion-anchoring discipline becomes more important, not less: when you must decline a powerful reviewer's suggestion, a reasoned reject grounded in an accessibility or alignment criterion is defensible in a way that \"I disagree\" is not. The transferable skill is responding to feedback with judgment when the reviewer outranks you, holding to a criterion rather than either capitulating or refusing, which is the situation most professional design review actually involves.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"A senior stakeholder wants a change your criterion contradicts\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"A senior client insists you replace the high-contrast button palette with their brand pastels, which would drop below the WCAG contrast threshold. They outrank you. How do you respond?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Comply immediately; they outrank you\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Capitulating to authority over a criterion produces an artifact that excludes some learners. Rank does not change whether the palette meets the accessibility standard.\"},{\"label\":\"Refuse flatly: \\\"I disagree, the current palette stays\\\"\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"\\\"I disagree\\\" is a preference clash with no criterion. Against a senior stakeholder it reads as obstinacy and you lose the argument.\"},{\"label\":\"Reject on the criterion: show the pastels fail the contrast standard and offer a brand-aligned palette that passes\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. A reasoned reject grounded in an accessibility criterion is defensible in a way personal disagreement is not, and offering a compliant alternative respects the brand goal.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"The pastel buttons ship. An accessibility audit later flags the contrast failure. Who owns it?\",\"feedback\":\"You do; you made the change. Deferring to rank did not transfer responsibility for the criterion you knew it violated.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"You refused without a criterion. The stakeholder escalates. What is the weakness of your position?\",\"feedback\":\"You framed a criterion question as a taste disagreement, so there is nothing reviewable to back you up. The reject had no anchor.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"You logged the reject with the contrast standard and a passing alternative. How does this land?\",\"feedback\":\"As professional judgment: you held to a checkable criterion, not your preference, and gave the stakeholder a path that meets both the brand and the standard. The disposition is defensible to anyone who reads it.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the peer-critique-and-disposition log (criterion-anchored critique points plus accept / modify / reject dispositions) packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/06-06-peer-scholarly-critique-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"peer-critique-and-disposition log (criterion-anchored critique points plus accept / modify / reject dispositions)\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"06.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the peer-critique-and-disposition log (criterion-anchored critique points plus accept / modify / reject dispositions).\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the peer-critique-and-disposition log (criterion-anchored critique points plus accept / modify / reject dispositions) for AIL 606 06.06. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_peer_scholarly_critique\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm your critique anchors every point to a criterion, labels preferences as preferences, and that your response log gives each point a reasoned disposition including at least one reject. Applause and compliance are not scholarly critique.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"peer_review","stem":"A peer review of a classmate's prototype reads in full: 'Great work, very clear and professional. 5/5.' Why does this fall short of scholarly critique?","options":["It is too short to be polite.","It is praise with a rating but names no specific element, problem, or criterion the author can act on.","It should not include a numeric rating.","It uses the word professional."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Scholarly critique identifies a specific element, the problem with it, and the criterion behind the judgment; encouragement alone is not critique.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_6_praise_as_critique","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["peer_review"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-01","ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-04"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"peer_review","stem":"Which peer comment gives the author the most actionable, defensible feedback?","options":["I would have picked a different color scheme; blue feels nicer to me.","The Module 2 objective says 'evaluate,' but the matching activity only asks learners to recall definitions, so the assessment is misaligned.","This is not really my style.","I think most people would prefer a video here."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Tying critique to a shared criterion (objective-to-assessment alignment) lets the author judge the point on its merits, unlike a preference statement.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_6_opinion_without_criterion","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["peer_review"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI to draft a critique of a peer's storyboard and it lists a 'missing accessibility statement' the storyboard does not actually have a place for. What is the correct move before submitting?","options":["Submit the AI critique as written; it sounds thorough.","Verify each flagged issue against the peer's actual artifact and remove or correct points that are not real.","Add even more AI-generated points to look rigorous.","Tell the peer the AI found the problems, so they are not your responsibility."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An unverified AI critique can fabricate issues; you must confirm each point against the real artifact before it becomes your review.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_6_ai_critique_unverified","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-03"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_6_peer_scholarly_critique_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"You received eight peer comments. A reviewer says your response 'shows no judgment' because you applied all eight edits without comment. What practice fixes this?","options":["Apply none of the comments instead.","Record a disposition for each comment, accept, modify, or reject, with a one-line reason.","Ask the peers to rewrite their comments.","Only respond to the comments from the highest-rated peer."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Logging a reasoned accept, modify, or reject per comment shows that peer review informed your judgment rather than replacing it.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_6_critique_without_disposition","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-06-06-lo-02"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_6_praise_as_critique","pattern":"praise_as_critique","description":"Submitting peer feedback that is mostly encouragement ('great job, very clear') with no actionable, evidence-based observation","trigger":"review consists of compliments and a rating with no specific design issue","feedback":"A scholarly critique names a specific element, the problem with it, and a criterion or principle behind the judgment. Praise is not critique.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_6_opinion_without_criterion","pattern":"opinion_without_criterion","description":"Phrasing critique as personal preference ('I would have used blue') instead of tying it to a shared rubric, principle, or learner outcome","trigger":"feedback uses 'I don't like' with no criterion the author can act on objectively","feedback":"Reframe preferences as criteria: name the alignment, usability, or accessibility standard, so the author can judge the point on its merits.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_6_critique_without_disposition","pattern":"critique_without_disposition","description":"Receiving peer critique and either accepting every comment or dismissing it wholesale, rather than recording a reasoned accept, modify, or reject disposition for each point","trigger":"author makes all suggested edits or none, with no rationale logged per comment","feedback":"Log a disposition for each comment: accept, modify, or reject, with the reason. Peer review informs judgment; it does not replace it.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_6_ai_critique_unverified","pattern":"ai_critique_unverified","description":"Submitting AI-generated critique of a peer's work as your own review without verifying that the issues are real and present in the artifact","trigger":"learner pastes an AI critique that flags problems not actually in the peer's prototype","feedback":"If you use AI to draft critique, verify each issue against the peer's actual artifact before submitting; an unchecked AI review can fabricate problems.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"17bf558d36d0ad8e530c3704e3ac5ceea2cb239d3a928f9305acf429478db1e5","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}